    "text": "96\n\n The Real Truth Behind the Buying \"Boring\" Businesses Clickbait\nHi Sara,\nIf you've spent even five minutes on YouTube this year, keep reading. If you do not consume that gross social media content, please go back to enjoying your classy life reading hardbound classic literature and playing your pristine vinyl collection of classic music.\u00a0\nNow that it\u2019s just us trashy YouTube consumers left, let\u2019s speak freely.\nYou\u2019ve probably seen the trend: videos promising the dream of buying a \u201cboring business\u201d like an HVAC company, car wash, or plumbing service that practically prints money while you lounge on a yacht.\nI hate to burst the bubble, but as someone who\u2019s been knee-deep in the mergers and acquisitions world for over a decade, I\u2019m here to set the record straight.\nYes, these businesses can be profitable. No, they\u2019re not as \u201chands-off\u201d as YouTube makes them sound.\nHere\u2019s the truth:\nThey\u2019re People-Intensive\nThese businesses thrive on their teams\u2014but people also mean overhead and drama. Think payroll, health insurance, workman's comp, and endless management challenges. Scaling up? That just adds more moving parts. People have feelings and emergencies and generally require a lot of resources.\n\nCustomer Service Isn\u2019t Optional\nWhether it\u2019s an HVAC technician or a car wash attendant, businesses that serve homeowners demand excellent customer service. And \u201cexcellent\u201d doesn\u2019t happen without constant oversight. Equipment breaks. Complaints roll in. Someone has to manage it all. A few negative reviews can quite literally bankrupt you.\n\nYou\u2019ll Need More Than a Dream\nMany first-time buyers think they\u2019ll ramp up marketing and \u201cmanage from afar.\u201d But unless you\u2019re a professional management company with an executive team in place, this approach can be a recipe for disaster. Good luck finding a great operator- no one wants to take a meager salary and run a business soup to nuts for some guy living in Manhattan.\u00a0\n\nEmployees Fear Change\nAfter an acquisition, employees often worry about layoffs or pay cuts. Stabilizing operations and winning their trust takes time\u2014and money. And time is money\u2026so you get the point.\n\nWas that discouraging? I\u2019m sorry! It can also be really great. If you\u2019re ready to roll up your sleeves and tackle the operational side of business ownership, these industries can be lucrative and rewarding. But walking in with rose-colored glasses and think you pull something off based on what you learned from a 90-second TikTok? That\u2019s a no-go.\nThe Best Kept Secret in the Business World\nA lot of people I talk to are looking to buy a business. A great portion of those folks tell me they are \u2018industry agnostic.\u2019 They are very open-minded, but it\u2019s also kind of an enormous thing to not narrow down. It\u2019s like saying you\u2019re \u2018degree agnostic\u2019 in college or \u2018career agnostic\u2019 after. Eventually, you have to decide or life will decide for you, right?\u00a0\nA ton of acquisition happens in-home services (the general term for stuff like HVAC, plumbers, and electricians), and as an asset class, those are a pretty mixed bag. However, one industry consistently flies under the radar: accounting practices. They\u2019re reasonably priced, have excellent margins (usually), and are often less resource-intensive than other businesses.\nHow many of us have looked for a new accountant only to be told that their accountant isn\u2019t taking new clients? In what other industry is that even a thing?\u00a0\nCurious to learn more about finding the right business to buy?\u00a0\nClick here to check out Deal Academy, where we have courses and trainings to support you in buying and selling accounting firms.\nAnd if you\u2019re not already subscribed to my YouTube channel, where I talk mergers, acquisitions, and everything in between, now\u2019s the perfect time.\u00a0\nWeekly tips from someone in the trenches every day? Yes, please.\nHere\u2019s to making smarter deals,\nSara Sharp\nDeal Attorney, Partner\nSK&S Law Group\n--\nFor more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2024\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
